<TopicPage slug="subjects/economics/advanced/development-economics">
  <h2>The idea</h2>
  <p>
    Development economics asks <strong>why some countries are poor and what actually lifts
    them</strong> — the field where economics meets its highest stakes. Its modern method:
    randomised controlled trials testing specific interventions (the Nobel-2019 approach),
    layered on older debates about aid, trade and institutions. The results have replaced
    confident ideology with careful, sometimes humbling, evidence.
  </p>

  <Widget no="1" caption="the record: what convergence achieved, what was learned, what remains" widget="equationStep" config={devCfg} />

  <h2>Lessons the field has earned</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><strong>Health first pays compounding dividends:</strong> deworming, malaria bednets and vaccination raise school attendance and lifetime earnings — cheap interventions with giant returns, once a champion (Bloomberg-era low-cost data, too) exists to deliver them.</li>
    <li><strong>Cash transfers work better than critics feared:</strong> direct money to poor households raises consumption, schooling and assets — and does not fuel the alcohol-and-idle-spending myth (meta-analyses across dozens of RCTs).</li>
    <li><strong>Aid debates split into answerable questions:</strong> "does aid work?" is too broad. "Does bednet distribution X at cost Y raise attendance Z?" is answerable — and was.</li>
    <li><strong>Institutions are the deep variable:</strong> geography and culture explain some divergence; the colonial-institutions research (Acemoglu, Johnson, Robinson, Nobel 2024) argues settler mortality shaped property rights, which shaped prosperity — contested, but the most influential hypothesis of the generation.</li>
  </ul>
  <p class="sources"><strong>Sources:</strong> Banerjee & Duflo, Poor Economics (2011); Acemoglu & Robinson, Why Nations Fail (2012); World Bank poverty data; Nobel citations 2019 & 2024.</p>
</TopicPage>
